// Oracle question: what does a bare identifier at the start of a statement do,
// and what happens when a store follows it on the same line?
//
// `Instr -> Arg` (`compiler.inc:154`) makes a lone operand a statement, and
// `dofunction` turns a section name into a call - so `wsync` on its own line
// is a call to the `inline` of that name.  K65 being newline-insensitive, the
// next thing on the line is simply the next statement: `wsync HMOVE = a` is a
// call and a store, and every attested A2600 source is written that way.
//
// K816 gave the *first* identifier of a statement to its address-encoding
// prefix rule - `dp`, `abs`, `far` take the operand that follows them - so any
// bare call with something after it on the line was `K816-E0001`.  Decision
// 16; fixed in G1's builder, where the prefix's spelling is visible and the
// grammar's is not (`postmortem.md` §G.1.8).  The manifest row is `instr-call`
// and this is what closes it.
//
// Three shapes: a call with a store after it, a call alone on its line, and
// two calls on one line - the last because "the line ends the statement" is
// exactly the rule that is *not* being introduced here.
//
// Each `inline` stores rather than setting a flag, so that the order of the
// expansions survives the optimizer: a flag written and rewritten is a dead
// write and pass 3 is right to drop it, which would leave this fixture
// asserting nothing about order.

const MARK = 0x5A

var slot = 0x80
var one  = 0x81
var two  = 0x82

inline stamp_one { one = a }
inline stamp_two { two = a }

data answers { MARK }

main {
  a = slot
  stamp_one slot = a
  stamp_one
  stamp_two stamp_one
  a = answers
  return
}
